      "html": "<button class=\"ds-web-accordion\" aria-expanded=\"false\"><span><strong>Allergen Preferences</strong><small>Track allergens you want to be warned about in foods</small></span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m6 9 6 6 6-6\"/></svg></button>",
      "css": ".ds-web-accordion{width:100%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:16px;padding:16px;border:1px solid hsl(40 22% 82%);border-radius:8px;background:transparent;color:hsl(213 19% 11%);text-align:left;cursor:pointer}.ds-web-accordion:hover{background:hsl(39 27% 89%)}.ds-web-accordion:focus-visible{outline:2px solid hsl(163 79% 21%);outline-offset:-2px}.ds-web-accordion span{display:grid;gap:4px}.ds-web-accordion strong{font-size:14px;font-weight:500}.ds-web-accordion small{font-size:14px;font-weight:400;color:hsl(153 12% 36%)}.ds-web-accordion svg{flex:none;transition:transform .2s}.ds-web-accordion[aria-expanded=true] svg{transform:rotate(180deg)}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Status overview plus clear sections",
    "overview": "This record describes the implemented Settings surfaces only: `SparkyFitnessMobile/src/screens/SettingsScreen.tsx`, `SparkyFitnessMobile/src/components/SettingsRow.tsx`, and `SparkyFitnessFrontend/src/pages/Settings/SettingsPage.tsx`. It is not a claim about every screen in either app. The Settings experience is an **Operate** surface: people check their current context, find a category, and reach a specific setting. The mobile screen leads with server and sync state. The web screen leads with the active profile and five named sections.\n\nThe light themes use warm cream backgrounds, darker text, and a restrained green accent; the dark themes keep the same semantic roles with darker surfaces and brighter green. Status copy reports observed connection and sync history, including checking and unavailable states. Sections and controls use the actual English locale strings, with translations supplied by each app's i18n system.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Grouped settings with headings that name the user's task.",
      "Current account or connection context near the top of the page.",
      "One destination per mobile row; category tabs and expandable settings on web.",
      "Visible text and accessibility labels for state, with color as a supporting cue."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Semantic State Rule",
        "body": "A connection dot may reinforce status, but text must state whether the connection is checking, connected, or unavailable. Sync history must also expose its loading, missing, and unavailable states in words.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Scan First Rule",
        "body": "Keep section names and setting titles stronger than explanatory text. Preserve meaningful labels when they wrap; the mobile row title has no line limit by default.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Destination Rule",
        "body": "Keep a category label, its controls, and deep-link section IDs aligned when changing the Settings layout. Mobile rows continue to navigate to their existing screens.",
        "section": "layout"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put current context before the detailed controls on these Settings surfaces.",
      "Do keep labels task-oriented and use the English locale entries as the copy source.",
      "Do keep connection and sync information truthful in loading and error states.",
      "Do preserve accessible names, focus indicators, navigation destinations, and URL section aliases."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't present a color dot as the only status indicator.",
      "Don't invent account, connection, sync, or health status from decorative UI.",
      "Don't collapse unrelated settings into one unlabelled group."
    ]
  }
}
